From cbd491be7f3a1a75d44d97867f0379e404a772be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:54:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: native output on nvidia-drm puts NVIDIA's
 depth and colour format at "auto", and their end gives them back

Driver 620 has two connector properties of its own on HDMI connectors,
NV_CONNECTOR_OUTPUT_BPC and NV_CONNECTOR_COLOR_FORMAT, which ask NVKMS for
a depth and a format whatever "max bpc" says. The transport set
NV_CONNECTOR_COLOR_FORMAT to "auto" but did not know
NV_CONNECTOR_OUTPUT_BPC: a value of it that another program left (10, say)
sent the Dolby Vision tunnel at that depth with "max bpc" 8. With
nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9062 the driver refuses a connector state with
Dolby Vision unless both are "auto" (or 8 and RGB), so such a value would
keep native output from starting at all.

The transport sets NV_CONNECTOR_OUTPUT_BPC to "auto" too, where the
connector has it, so that "max bpc" 8 decides the depth
(nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9000). Both are settings to its end (PartOf(),
MODE_SETTING, as a depth or a format that a mode may not carry): what was
found, what the user chose in the settings or another program left, goes
back in the commit that ends the signals, and stays at "auto" where the
driver will not take it back. Kodi itself sets neither of them for its
depth (that goes through "max bpc"); NV_CONNECTOR_COLOR_FORMAT is the
property of its colour format on nvidia-drm, put back by its settings
with the next change of mode. Low-Latency and the HDMI 1.4b signalling
need the same (the tunnel is RGB at 8 bpc in all three, and 9062 checks
all three; the AVI InfoFrame of YCbCr 4:2:2 of Low-Latency is made by NVKMS
from NV_HDMI_DV_LOW_LATENCY, not asked through the colour format). HDR
over the tunnel is the kernel's backends' only.

What the transport sets moves to TransportSpecs.h, without DRM, so that a
test on the host builds the list that TakeOver() uses: with a model of the
driver of 9062 and an output with YCbCr 4:4:4 and 10 bits, Standard,
Low-Latency and HDMI 1.4b start with both at "auto" and end with both as
found; driver 615 needs neither; the kernel's backends get none of
NVIDIA's. 642 checks; leaving out either property fails it.
